                <pb facs="0821" n="723" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg723"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/1762: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at Harbin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Jenkins</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-10-21T15:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 21,
                                1920—3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>For Stevens:</p>
                <p>Meeting of Consortium delegates October 15th New York. Extract from Minutes:
                    [Here follows the Minute quoted in Mr. Lamont’s letter of October 18, printed
                        <hi rendition="#italic">supra.</hi>]</p>
                <p>Department instructing Legation, Peking, to repeat its 319, October 13, 6
                        p.m.<anchor n="74" xml:id="d745-f1"/> to you for your information, wherein
                    Legation states it feels assured Chinese Government does not intend to
                    discontinue Inter-Allied provisional control.</p>
                <p>Your October 6, 10 a.m.<anchor n="75" xml:id="d745-f2"/> It is not contemplated
                    that you should return to Harbin under the present arrangement. It is desired
                    that you come to Washington for conference and assist in devising a new and
                    better plan. Very important that no idea that United States is withdrawing be
                    created by your departure.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Colby</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
